{"cuisine/cuisine-of-philadelphia":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-philadelphia","n":"Cuisine of Philadelphia","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, United States, was shaped largely by the city's mixture of ethnicities, available foodstuffs and history. Certain foods have become associated with the city."],["Invented in Philadelphia in the 1930s, the cheesesteak is the most well known, and soft pretzels have long been a major part of Philadelphia culture."],["The late 19th and early 20th centuries saw the creation of two Philadelphia landmarks offering an array of food options, the Reading Terminal Market and the Italian Market. After a dismal restaurant scene during the post-war era of the 20th century, the 1970s brought a restaurant renaissance that has continued into the 21st century."],["Many foods and drinks associated with Philadelphia can also commonly be linked with Pennsylvania Dutch cuisine and Italian-American cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-philadelphia"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Philadelphia"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Philadelphia Chef's Table: Extraordinary Recipes from the City of Brotherly Love","ref":"cookbook/the-philadelphia-chef-s-table-extraordinary-recipes-from-the-city-of-brotherly-love","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"April White","note":"Recipes from Philadelphia restaurants and chefs.","url":"https://www.simonandschuster.ca/books/Philadelphia-Chefs-Table/April-White/Chefs-Table/9780762789443","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780762789443"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/nicaraguan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nicaraguan-cuisine","n":"Nicaraguan cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Nicaragua","intro":[["Nicaraguan cuisine includes a mixture of Mesoamerican, Chibcha, Spanish, Caribbean, and African cuisine. Despite the blending and incorporation of pre-Columbian, Spanish and African influences, traditional cuisine differs from the western half of Nicaragua to the eastern half. Western Nicaraguan cuisine revolves around the Mesoamerican diet of the Chorotega and Nicarao people such as maize, tomatoes, avocados, turkey, squash, beans, chili, and chocolate, in addition to potatoes which were cultivated by the Chibcha people originating from South America and introduced meats like pork and chicken. Eastern Nicaraguan cuisine consists mostly of seafood and coconut."],["The national dish of Nicaragua is gallo pinto."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nicaraguan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicaraguan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Nicaraguan Cooking: My Grandmother's Recipes","ref":"cookbook/nicaraguan-cooking-my-grandmother-s-recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Trudy Espinoza-Abrams, 2004","note":"Family recipes of traditional Nicaraguan dishes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781413437799","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781413437799"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Vigorón","dish/vigoron"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chicharrón","ingredient/chicharron"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/30/Indio-viejo-nicaraguense.jpg/500px-Indio-viejo-nicaraguense.jpg","w":500,"h":380,"by":"MiguelRamirez77","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indio-viejo-nicaraguense.jpg"}},"cuisine/chinese-buddhist-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chinese-buddhist-cuisine","n":"Chinese Buddhist cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Buddhist cuisine is an Asian cuisine that is followed by monks and many believers from areas historically influenced by Mahayana Buddhism. It is vegetarian or vegan, and it is based on the Dharmic concept of ahimsa (non-violence). Vegetarianism is common in other Dharmic faiths such as Hinduism, Jainism and Sikhism, as well as East Asian religions, like Taoism. While monks, nuns and a minority of believers are vegetarian year-round, many believers follow the Buddhist vegetarian diet for celebrations."],["In Buddhism, cooking is often seen as a spiritual practice that produces the nourishment which the body needs to work hard and meditate. The origin of \"Buddhist food\" as a distinct sub-style of cuisine is tied to monasteries, where one member of the community would have the duty of being the head cook and supplying meals that paid respect to the strictures of Buddhist precepts. Temples that were open to visitors from the general public might also serve meals to them and a few temples effectively run functioning restaurants on the premises. In Japan, this culinary custom, recognized as shōjin ryōri or devotion cuisine, is commonly offered at numerous temples, notably in Kyoto. This centuries-old culinary tradition, primarily associated with religious contexts, is seldom encountered beyond places like temples, religious festivals, and funerals. A more recent version, more Chinese in style, is prepared by the Ōbaku school of zen, and known as fucha ryōri (普茶料理); this is served at the head temple of Manpuku-ji, as well as various subtemples. In modern times, commercial restaurants have also latched on to the style, catering both to practicing and non-practicing lay people."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chinese-buddhist-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Buddhist_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Vegan Chinese Kitchen: Recipes and Modern Stories from a Thousand-Year-Old Tradition","ref":"cookbook/the-vegan-chinese-kitchen-recipes-and-modern-stories-from-a-thousand-year-old-tradition","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Hannah Che, 2022","note":"Plant-based Chinese cooking rooted in the Buddhist vegetarian tradition, by a Chinese American cook.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780593139707","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780593139707"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Kajitsu","dish/kajitsu"],["Saikan","dish/saikan"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/2/2d/Chinese-buddhist-cuisine-taiwan-1.jpg","w":500,"h":378,"by":"Pratyeka at the English-language Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese-buddhist-cuisine-taiwan-1.jpg"}},"cuisine/wayuu-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/wayuu-foodways","n":"Wayuu foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, La Guajira Peninsula, Colombia and Venezuela","names":"Wayuu","intro":[["The Wayuu, the largest Indigenous people in Colombia, cook from both the sea and the dry land of La Guajira: fish and shellfish, ",["corn","ingredient/maize"],", beans, squash, ",["yuca","ingredient/cassava"],", and ",["goat","ingredient/goat"]," above all. Friche, goat meat and offal cooked in its own juices and then fried in its fat, is made for special occasions and to honor guests, with grilled arepa, bollo or boiled yuca. The fogón, the kitchen fire, is meant to stay lit and is where the family gathers."],["Corn drinks feed the family: ookostuushi, a corn mazamorra for children, and uujolu, a ",["chicha","dish/chicha"]," without alcohol, for hot days and for marriage proposals. In Manaure, Wayuu families grow kapeshuna, a small bean sown before the rains, and women save the seed in gourds until the next season. It goes into poy, a thick soup with yellow corn and lamb fat."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Radio Nacional de Colombia, Gastronomía wayuu, platos típicos","url":"https://radionacional.co/cultura/tradiciones/gastronomia-wayuu-platos-tipicos-de-su-gastronomia"},{"name":"Slow Food Foundation, La Guajira cowpea Presidium","url":"https://www.fondazioneslowfood.com/en/slow-food-presidia/la-guajira-bean/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"wayuu-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Chicha","dish/chicha"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/1/17/Chicha_with_Pipe%C3%B1o.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"simenon simenon","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chicha_with_Pipe%C3%B1o.jpg","of":"Chicha"}},"ingredient/turmeric":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/turmeric","n":"Turmeric","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 21 dishes across 13 cuisines","intro":[["Turmeric (), or Curcuma longa (), is a flowering plant in the ginger family Zingiberaceae. It is a perennial, rhizomatous, herbaceous plant native to the Indian subcontinent and Southeast Asia that requires temperatures between 20 and 30 °C (68 and 86 °F) and high annual rainfall to thrive. Plants are gathered each year for their rhizomes, some for propagation in the following season and some for consumption or dyeing."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-turmeric"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turmeric"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":13,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Gulai","dish/gulai"],["Manuk napinadar","dish/manuk-napinadar"],["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"],["Mie ayam","dish/mie-ayam"],["Nasi kuning","dish/nasi-kuning"],["Otak-otak","dish/otak-otak"],["Rendang","dish/rendang"],["Rujak soto","dish/rujak-soto"],["Saksang","dish/saksang"],["Sate lilit","dish/sate-lilit"],["Soto ayam","dish/soto-ayam"],["Tahu campur","dish/tahu-campur"],["Udang balado","dish/udang-balado"]]],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"],["Piyanggang manok","dish/piyanggang-manok"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"],["Piyanggang manok","dish/piyanggang-manok"]]],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food",[["Gulai","dish/gulai"],["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"],["Piyanggang manok","dish/piyanggang-manok"]]],["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"]]],["Balinese cuisine","cuisine/balinese-cuisine",[["Sate lilit","dish/sate-lilit"]]],["Bangladeshi cuisine","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine",[["Shorshe ilish","dish/shorshe-ilish"]]],["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",[["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"]]],["Jewish c